  "client_view_text": "  When the database is operating normally, the transaction log is associated with the current database file.  During a crash, the database file is not consistent, but the log contains information about the operations that were pending when the crash occurred.\nA write-ahead log allows a database to recover from crashes by recording all changes to the database in a separate log file that is written before any data is written to the database file.  When a crash occurs, the database file is not consistent, but the log file contains information about the operations that were pending at the time of the crash.  The database can then use the log file to recover the database to a consistent state by replaying the operations recorded in the log.\nHere is the correct answer.\nA write-ahead log allows a database to recover from crashes by recording all changes to the database in a separate log file that is written before any data is written to the database file.  When a crash occurs, the database file is not consistent, but the log file contains information about the operations that were pending at the time of the crash.  The database can then use the log file to recover the database to a consistent state by replaying the operations recorded in the log. \nNote:  This is a very basic answer and might need"
